"# Accessibility\n\nNodus takes care of each component's accessibility: ARIA roles, keyboard, focus, contrast. The\napplication remains responsible for the page that puts them together: structure, labels,\nannouncements. This guide separates the two.\n\n## 1. What Nodus guarantees\n\nMost of these points are checked by tests run on every build (full list:\n[guarantees table](../../../SPECS.md)).\n\n| Guarantee | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| **Valid ARIA** | Compliant roles and attributes (`aria-expanded`, `aria-selected`, `aria-sort`…). An overlay trigger is always focusable. |\n| **Keyboard** | Each interactive component follows the matching WAI-ARIA pattern. The **Keyboard** section of each component page lists the keys. |\n| **Focus** | Visible ring everywhere (`--color-ring`); focus trapped in Dialog, Sheet, Drawer and AlertDialog, then returned to the trigger on close; a single tab stop per group (tabs, radio buttons, toggle groups). |\n| **Contrast** | Text ≥ 4.5:1 and control outlines ≥ 3:1 on every surface, in light and dark (default values, WCAG 2). Text pairs also meet an APCA threshold, closer to perceived legibility in dark mode: Lc 75 for body text, Lc 60 for secondary text. |\n| **Motion** | Under `prefers-reduced-motion`, animation and transition durations drop to zero. |\n| **Composite fields** | Combobox, MultiSelect, DatePicker and DateRangePicker get their accessible name from `Field`, like a simple field. |\n| **Toasts** | Announced to screen readers (`status`, or `alert` for an error); the countdown pauses on hover and while focus is inside the toast. |\n\n## 2. What the application must do\n\n### Page structure\n\n- A skip link first (`SkipLink()`, \"Skip to content\"), a `<header>` (`AppHeader`), a labelled\n  `<nav>`, a `<main id=\"main\">` with a single `<h1>`. A starter application has no footer (brand\n  charter B, since 4.24.0): version, data and licence live on the \"About\" page of the ⋯ menu; a\n  site that has one writes it with `AppFooter` (`<footer>`). With a hash router,\n  `SkipLink` needs its behavior: it focuses `<main>` without changing the route.\n- A card clickable as a whole carries its link in its title: `CardTitle({ href })`. Never wrap a\n  card in an `<a>` (all its text would become the link name).\n- A heading hierarchy without gaps: `CardTitle({ level })` sets the level of a card title.\n\n### Labels\n\n- **Every field has a label**, through `Field`:\n\n```js\nField({ label: 'Email address', id: 'email', children: Input({ id: 'email', type: 'email' }) })\n```\n\n  `Input({ label })` does not exist: the prop is silently ignored. A field without a visible label\n  (search, table cell) keeps its label, hidden on screen: `Field({ labelPos: 'hidden' })`.\n- **A button without text has a name**:\n\n```js\nButton({ size: 'icon', iconStart: Icon('x'), attrs: { 'aria-label': 'Close' } })\n```\n\n- **An icon that carries meaning** gets a `label` (`Icon('alert-triangle', { label: 'Warning' })`);\n  without `label`, it is decorative and hidden from screen readers.\n- An `Avatar` image gets an `alt`; its initials (`fallback`) do not replace a name.\n\n### Announcements\n\nA change visible elsewhere than under the focus (deleted item, filtered list, save) must be\nannounced. A toast does it; otherwise:\n\n```js\nimport { announcer } from 'nodus';\nannouncer.announce('3 items deleted');\n```\n\n### Focus after an action\n\nWhen a focused element disappears (deleted row, panel closed outside an overlay), move the focus\nto a neighbouring element with Reef's `focus()`. Otherwise it falls back to `<body>` and keyboard\nusers start again from the top of the page. The full recipe is in\n[lists and nested structures](../../../GUIDELINES.md#listes-et-structures-imbriquées-depuis-310).\n\n### Navigation or action\n\nA navigation (another page, another route) is a link: `ButtonLink`, even if it looks like a\nbutton. An action on the page is a `Button`. A button that changes `location` loses middle-click,\n\"copy link\" and the \"link\" announcement.\n\n## 3. Checking\n\n1. **Keyboard only**: every flow works without a mouse (Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter, Space, Escape,\n   arrows), and the focus stays visible.\n2. **With a screen reader** (VoiceOver, NVDA): every control is announced with its name, role and\n   state. The VoiceOver method is detailed below.\n3. **In dark mode and at 200 % zoom**: nothing disappears or overlaps.\n4. **With a colour override**: the [Colours and surfaces](#/foundations/colors) page computes the\n   contrast in both themes, with WCAG 2 and APCA.\n\n## 4. Testing with VoiceOver\n\nVoiceOver is Apple's screen reader, built into macOS and iOS. Test in **Safari**: it is the pairing\nVoiceOver users rely on, and Chrome does not always render the same way.\n\n### Turning it on\n\n- **macOS**: `Cmd + F5`, or press Touch ID three times. The **VO** key means `Ctrl + Option`. On\n  first launch, dismiss the quick start; in VoiceOver Utility, note the verbosity and language,\n  which change the announcements.\n- **iOS**: Settings › Accessibility › VoiceOver. Add it to the accessibility shortcut (triple-click\n  the side button) to toggle it quickly. An iPhone on the same network as the Mac opens the\n  development server through the Mac's IP address.\n\n### Essential commands on macOS\n\n| Keys | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| `VO + →` / `VO + ←` | Next / previous item (the VoiceOver cursor, distinct from keyboard focus) |\n| `Tab` / `Shift + Tab` | Next / previous control: the keyboard-only path |\n| `VO + Space` | Activate the item under the cursor |\n| `VO + U` | Rotor: lists of headings, links, landmarks, form controls; `←` / `→` switches list |\n| `VO + Cmd + H` | Next heading |\n| `VO + Shift + ↓` / `VO + Shift + ↑` | Interact with a group (table, list, toolbar) / stop interacting |\n| `VO + A` | Read from the cursor |\n| `Ctrl` | Stop speaking |\n\n### Gestures on iOS\n\n| Gesture | Effect |\n|---|---|\n| Swipe right / left | Next / previous item |\n| Double-tap | Activate |\n| Rotate two fingers | Pick the rotor setting (headings, landmarks, form controls…) |\n| Swipe down / up | Next / previous item by the rotor setting |\n| Two-finger scrub (Z shape) | Close or go back (the Escape equivalent) |\n| Two-finger tap | Stop speaking |\n\n### What to check\n\n- **Landmarks**: the \"Landmarks\" rotor lists the banner (`AppHeader`), the navigation, the main\n  content, the footer if there is one (`AppFooter`) and each search (`SearchField`). Two landmarks of the same type\n  each have a name.\n- **Headings**: the \"Headings\" rotor shows a single level-1 heading and a hierarchy without gaps; it\n  works as a table of contents.\n- **Fields**: each is announced by its label then its type (\"Email address, edit text\"), followed\n  by its hint and its error; never by the placeholder alone.\n- **Controls**: name, role and state — \"selected\", \"expanded\", \"checked\", \"dimmed\" for a disabled\n  control. An icon-only button says its name, never just \"button\" or \"image\".\n- **Overlays**: on opening, VoiceOver reads the dialog title; the cursor does not leave the panel;\n  on closing, focus returns to the trigger.\n- **Announcements**: a toast, `announcer.announce()` or a `SearchField` status is read without\n  moving focus.\n- **Reading order**: it follows the visual order; no hidden element is read.\n\n### Expected announcements, component by component\n\nThe **VoiceOver** section of each component page lists what VoiceOver should say in each state:\ncompare it with what you hear. These phrases come from the component documentation\n(`@voiceover`); a difference points to a defect in Nodus or in how the page assembles it.\n\n### Known limits\n\n- The busy state (`aria-busy`) is not always read: a loading `Button` is announced as \"dimmed\", and\n  the page must announce the end of loading (toast or `announcer`).\n- A live region created at the moment it fills is not always read. Nodus regions (`announcer`,\n  `SearchField` status) exist from the first render: keep them mounted.\n- Announcements vary with the system version, language and verbosity: record them with each test\n  result.\n\nGoing further: the **Accessibility** section of each component page, and the\n[Field rule](../../../GUIDELINES.md#règle-field--tout-input-a-un-label).\n"